From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lef <me@lefteros.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 06:58:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: google: zuma-husky: add the ST54L NFC
 controller, disabled

An nfc@8 child of hsi2c_0 (the haptics' bus), status "disabled", so a
normal boot is unchanged. The ST54L speaks plain NCI over I2C, so it takes
the same compatible as the 9a and the zumapro-tegu node: st,st54l with
nxp,nxp-nci-i2c as the fallback, for the nxp-nci driver. Pins are from the
stock device tree: interrupt gpa7-6, enable (the chip's RST_N) gph1-6,
power-idle gph1-3, with stock's two pin groups (power-down state only,
no pin function). The enable line is the same pin the zumapro-tegu node
uses.

The stock node also has a PMU clock pad (CLKOUT1) as the reference clock.
This commit has no clocks property, so the pad stays as the bootloader
leaves it. The power-idle line has no nxp-nci property; only its pin
group is kept. The eSE half of the chip is not described.

I tried it on my Pixel 8 Pro: a test kernel (7.3-rc6,
...-ge98a7a38a3ef) with the node switched on by an overlay, and the pad
clock from the follow-up patches either present or left out. nxp-nci
binds the ST54L and registers nfc0 either way. Without the clock the
NCI power-on works (the chip answers), but a sticker tag held at the
phone was not found in one 25 s run. With the clock a bus card (ISO-DEP,
no NDEF data on it) and the sticker tag (Type 2, one NDEF record) were
both found, and neard published both as tags. The no-clock result is a
single run, and I cannot read the PMU pad register from userspace, so I
do not know whether the bootloader leaves the pad partly set up. The
clock is in the follow-up patches. Not tested: the eSE, or s2idle with
the driver bound.

Signed-off-by: Lef <me@lefteros.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts     | 36 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts
index 478a57697..68b784336 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts
@@ -571,6 +571,20 @@ bluetooth {
  */
 &pinctrl_hsi1 {
 	status = "okay";
+
+	nfc_en: nfc-en-pins {
+		samsung,pins = "gph1-6";
+		samsung,pin-pud = <GS101_PIN_PULL_NONE>;
+		samsung,pin-con-pdn = <GS101_PIN_PDN_PREV>;
+		samsung,pin-pud-pdn = <GS101_PIN_PULL_NONE>;
+	};
+
+	nfc_pidle: nfc-pidle-pins {
+		samsung,pins = "gph1-3";
+		samsung,pin-pud = <GS101_PIN_PULL_DOWN>;
+		samsung,pin-con-pdn = <GS101_PIN_PDN_PREV>;
+		samsung,pin-pud-pdn = <GS101_PIN_PULL_DOWN>;
+	};
 };
 
 &usi0 {
@@ -594,6 +608,28 @@ haptics: haptic-driver@43 {
 		cirrus,lra-redc-micro-ohms = <13000000>;
 		cirrus,vpbr-threshold-microvolt = <3008000>;
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * The ST54L NFC controller at 0x08 on this bus: plain NCI over I2C,
+	 * nxp-nci's transport, as on the 9a and the zumapro-tegu node. Pins
+	 * from the stock DT: interrupt gpa7-6, enable (the chip's RST_N)
+	 * gph1-6, power-idle gph1-3. The pin groups are stock's and set only
+	 * the power-down state (and the power-idle pull-down), no pin
+	 * function. The chip's reference clock (stock: PMU pad CLKOUT1) has no
+	 * provider on zuma yet, so there is no clocks property and the pad
+	 * stays as the bootloader left it. The eSE half of the chip (spi_12,
+	 * reset gph1-5) is not described. Disabled: nothing binds to it until
+	 * a bring-up overlay enables it.
+	 */
+	nfc: nfc@8 {
+		compatible = "st,st54l", "nxp,nxp-nci-i2c";
+		reg = <0x08>;
+		interrupts-extended = <&gpa7 6 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		enable-gpios = <&gph1 6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		pinctrl-0 = <&nfc_en &nfc_pidle>;
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
 };
 
